The check is the same `isTTY` property from the last lesson, applied to `process.stdin` instead of `process.stdout`:

```js
import prompts from 'prompts';

async function resolveInvoiceId(idArg, invoices) {
  if (idArg) return idArg;
  if (!process.stdin.isTTY) {
    console.error('Error: invoice ID required (no ID given and no terminal to prompt).');
    process.exit(1);
  }
  const { id } = await prompts({
    type: 'select',
    name: 'id',
    message: 'Which invoice?',
    choices: invoices.map((i) => ({ title: i.summary, value: i.id })),
  });
  return id;
}
```

If `process.stdin.isTTY` is falsy — piped input, a CI runner, a cron job — the tool fails immediately with a specific, actionable error instead of silently waiting for an answer that will never come. That failure is far more useful than a three-hour timeout, because it tells the person debugging it exactly what was missing.

```js
program
  .command('delete-client <name>')
  .option('-y, --yes', 'Skip the confirmation prompt')
  .action(async (name, options) => {
    if (!options.yes) {
      if (!process.stdin.isTTY) {
        console.error('Error: --yes is required to delete a client from a non-interactive session.');
        process.exit(1);
      }
      const { confirmed } = await prompts({
        type: 'confirm',
        name: 'confirmed',
        message: `Delete client "${name}"? This can't be undone.`,
      });
      if (!confirmed) process.exit(1);
    }
    await deleteClient(name);
    console.log(`Deleted client "${name}".`);
  });
```

Three cases are all handled: a human at a terminal without `--yes` gets prompted; a human (or script) that already passed `--yes` skips straight to the action; and a non-interactive session without `--yes` fails immediately and explicitly, rather than hanging on a prompt nobody can answer. That third branch is what turns "invtrack sometimes just hangs" into "invtrack told me exactly what flag I forgot."

Every mainstream OS has a documented convention for exactly this split. On Linux it's the XDG Base Directory spec: `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` (default `~/.config`), `$XDG_CACHE_HOME` (default `~/.cache`), `$XDG_DATA_HOME` (default `~/.local/share`). macOS and Windows have their own equivalents. Rather than hand-rolling the per-OS logic, the `env-paths` package resolves it in one call:

```js
import envPaths from 'env-paths';
const paths = envPaths('invtrack');

// Linux:   paths.config → ~/.config/invtrack
//          paths.cache  → ~/.cache/invtrack
//          paths.data   → ~/.local/share/invtrack
// macOS:   paths.config → ~/Library/Preferences/invtrack-nodejs
// Windows: paths.config → %APPDATA%\invtrack-nodejs\Config
```

One line, and invtrack's config, cache and data each land in the place a user's backup tool, cloud sync, and "clear my cache" button already know how to treat correctly — because those tools were built against the same convention.

The resolution itself is one line once the sources are collected — the discipline is making the order explicit and documented, not clever:

```js
function resolveProvider(flags, env, fileConfig, defaults) {
  return flags.provider ?? env.INVTRACK_PROVIDER ?? fileConfig.provider ?? defaults.provider;
}
```

What actually prevents the "silently uses the wrong provider" bug from the hook isn't the precedence order alone — it's giving the user a way to *see* which source won. A `invtrack config show` or `--show-config` debug command that prints each resolved value next to where it came from (`provider: postmark (from ~/.config/invtrack/config.toml)`) turns a silent mystery into a two-second check.

