```

Same model, same prompt, same eventual output — the difference is entirely about *when* the user sees something happening. The first token typically arrives well under a second in, which is the actual signal that fixes the Ctrl-C problem: not a faster total response, but visible progress almost immediately.

```js
async function explainInvoice(invoice, options) {
  const stream = client.messages.stream({ /* ...same request... */ });

  if (options.json) {
    // Buffer the full response — a script needs one valid, parseable object
    let text = '';
    for await (const event of stream) {
      if (event.type === 'content_block_delta' && event.delta.type === 'text_delta') text += event.delta.text;
    }
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ invoiceId: invoice.id, explanation: text }));
    return;
  }

  // Human-facing: stream progressively
  for await (const event of stream) {
    if (event.type === 'content_block_delta' && event.delta.type === 'text_delta') {
      process.stdout.write(event.delta.text);
    }
  }
  console.log();
}
```

Notice the same stream object drives both branches — streaming is still how the request happens either way, since it doesn't cost anything extra; the difference is purely whether the tokens get printed as they arrive or accumulated and emitted once, complete, at the end.

