```js
async function callClaudeWithRetry(request, { maxRetries = 2 } = {}) {
  const RETRYABLE = new Set([429, 500, 529]);
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await client.messages.create(request);
    } catch (err) {
      const status = err.status;
      if (!RETRYABLE.has(status) || attempt >= maxRetries) throw err;
      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500 * 2 ** attempt)); // exponential backoff
    }
  }
}
```

Exponential backoff (500ms, then 1s, then 2s) avoids hammering an already-struggling API with immediate retries, and the retry loop only ever fires for the status codes actually worth retrying — a 401 or 400 throws immediately on the first attempt, straight into the graceful-fallback handling from the first half of this lesson.

